#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608 :topic/difficulty 1.1964810343639471 :topic/id #uuid "5824a36d-203d-5834-aa63-4aceedf31f46" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :topic/math-academy-id 906 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050995}] :topic/title "Introduction to First-Order Linear ODEs"}]}
